    id: 'api-08', title: 'GraphQL Basics', topic: 'api', difficulty: 'advanced', duration: 5,
    description: 'Understand GraphQL - an alternative to REST for querying APIs.',
    concept: 'GraphQL is a query language for APIs that lets clients request exactly the data they need, nothing more. Instead of multiple REST endpoints, you have a single /graphql endpoint. Clients send a query describing what fields they want, and the server returns only those fields. This eliminates over-fetching and under-fetching.',
    syntaxExample: '# GraphQL Query (client sends this)\nquery {\n  user(id: 42) {\n    name\n    email\n    posts {\n      title\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n# GraphQL Response (server returns this)\n{\n  "data": {\n    "user": {\n      "name": "Alice",\n      "email": "alice@x.com",\n      "posts": [\n        { "title": "Hello World" },\n        { "title": "GraphQL 101" }\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Using GraphQL in React\nconst query = `\n  query GetUser($id: ID!) {\n    user(id: $id) { name, email }\n  }\n`;\nconst { data } = await fetch("/graphql", {\n  method: "POST",\n  body: JSON.stringify({ query, variables: { id: 42 } })\n}).then(r => r.json());',
    visualExplanation: 'REST vs GraphQL:\n\nREST:   /users/42 -> returns ALL user fields (over-fetching)\n        then /users/42/posts -> another request\n\nGraphQL: one request, one response with exactly the fields you asked for\n  query { user(id: 42) { name posts { title } } }',
    projectUsage: ['Complex data requirements', 'Mobile apps (bandwidth sensitive)', 'Microservices aggregation', 'Real-time subscriptions'],
